      <section id="legal-risks">
        <h2>The Legal Framework: Indian Copyright Laws &amp; Injunctions</h2>
        <p>To understand the operational realities of Filmy4wap, one must review Indian intellectual property legislation. Copyright piracy carries severe civil and criminal penalties under Indian legal statutes.</p>

        <div class="tree-box">
          <ul class="tree-list">
            <li>├── Section 51 (Copyright Act): Defines unauthorized distribution &amp; infringement</li>
            <li>├── Section 63 (Copyright Act): Specifies criminal penalties (Fines &amp; Imprisonment)</li>
            <li>└── Cinematograph Act (2023 Amendments): Targeted anti-piracy measures for digital media</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Section 51 of the Indian Copyright Act, 1957:</strong> Explicitly deems copyright infringed when any person, without a license from the copyright owner, reproduces, distributes, or publicly displays protected creative works.</li>
          <li><strong>Section 63 of the Indian Copyright Act:</strong> Outlines criminal offenses for knowingly infringing copyright, providing for penalties including mandatory imprisonment ranging from six months to three years, alongside fines starting at ₹50,000 up to ₹2,000,000.</li>
          <li><strong>The Cinematograph (Amendment) Act, 2023:</strong> Specifically targets unauthorized recording and digital dissemination of theatrical films, strengthening the legal mandate for swift site-blocking orders.</li>
          <li><strong>Dynamic "John Doe" Injunctions:</strong> High Courts in India routinely issue dynamic injunctions against piracy domain mirrors. When ISPs (Internet Service Providers) receive court orders, they block domain names at the DNS level, forcing administrators to constantly cycle through domain extensions (.in, .site, .tech) to stay online.</li>
        </ul>

